    "q-133808": {
      "dom_id": "question-133808",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Darryl gives $\\frac{2}{5}$ of his apple pies to his daughter. What fraction of a whole pie does Darryl's daughter have?\n\n*Remember that Darryl bought $\\frac{4}{3}$ of an apple pie.*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Darryl's daughter has\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nof a pie.",
      "worked_solution": "We know that Darryl has $\\frac{4}{3}$ of an apple pie.\n\nTo calculate the fraction of a whole pie that Darryl's daughter has, we need to multiply $\\frac{2}{5}$ by $\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{2\\times 4}{5\\times 3}=\\frac{8}{15}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Darry's daughter have $\\frac{8}{15}$ of a pie.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
